{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"REBAR-DEBUG PHASE 1 — confirmed root cause (correcting the original abort narrative)\n\nThe fixed `.cache.json.tmp` pathname is a real concurrency defect, but it does not make the immutable gate-snapshot fan-out abort: 16 barrier-synchronized real `reduce_ticket` calls produced 15 caught FileNotFoundError warnings, zero escaped exceptions, equal results, and a valid final cache. Repository logging was also proven non-throwing; only a deliberately rethrowing negative-control handler escaped.\n\nThe confirmed defect is more serious and broader: two reductions of different event generations open/truncate the same temp inode. One rename can expose that inode as `.cache.json` while another writer continues mutating it, splicing fields from independently valid `{dir_hash,state}` envelopes. A real reducer experiment appended an EDIT between eight old-generation and eight new-generation reductions. In 14 rounds it produced a final cache carrying the CURRENT generation hash but stale state (`authorship` remained signed=1/unsigned=0 instead of signed=1/unsigned=1). A subsequent normal `reduce_ticket` returned that wrong cached state while replay was replaced with a sentinel that would raise, proving a false cache hit. Thirty serialized control rounds produced zero false hits.\n\nRoot cause: `src/rebar/reducer/_cache.py:143` assigns every writer `cache_path + \".tmp\"`; `read_cache` validates the outer hash and attestation-kind parity but cannot detect ordinary fields spliced from another generation. This violates the function contract to atomically write one state envelope, the reducer-cache invalidation contract, and the event-sourced invariant that compiled state is deterministic replay. The original plan-review-abort explanation is rejected; the repaired ticket targets coherent atomic publication under concurrent, divergent writers."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786302639984111001,"uuid":"167a44e7-ddc1-4880-99d4-504f1998657f"}